<figure class="hot" data-r31-hot aria-labelledby="r31-hot-title">
 <figcaption id="r31-hot-title"><span>Figure 2</span> When can the index keep its original address?</figcaption>
 <label>Update case <select data-case><option value="hot">Price not indexed · same-page space</option><option value="indexed">Price indexed · same-page space</option><option value="full">Price not indexed · page full</option></select></label>
 <div class="drawing" data-live>
  <div class="index"><strong>Primary-key index</strong><p data-index>P7 → (0,1)</p></div>
  <div class="page"><strong>Heap page 0</strong><div class="tuple">Item 1 · P7 · 20.00 <span data-old>current version</span></div><div class="tuple replacement" data-new hidden></div></div>
  <div class="page second" data-page1 hidden><strong>Heap page 1</strong><div class="tuple">Item 1 · P7 · 25.00</div></div>
 </div>
 <p data-explanation data-live aria-live="polite">The primary-key entry leads to item 1. P7 costs 20.00.</p>
 <div class="controls" role="group" aria-label="Row version playback"><button data-play>Play</button><button data-back>Back</button><button data-next>Next</button><button data-reset>Reset</button><span data-step></span></div>
 <div data-hold aria-hidden="true"><span></span></div>
 <details><summary>Read the three outcomes as text</summary><p>With room on the page and no ordinary index referring to price, the new version is heap-only: the original index entry follows a same-page chain. Indexing price prevents HOT even though both versions fit together. Without page space, the new version lands on another page and ordinary indexes need new entries even though their key values did not change.</p></details>
 <p class="caption">Conceptual layout using the native experiment’s addresses. The new price becomes visible to fresh snapshots after commit. A report retaining an earlier Repeatable Read snapshot still sees 20.00.</p>
</figure>
<script>
import {stepper} from '../../figures/stepper.mjs';
for(const root of document.querySelectorAll('[data-r31-hot]')){
 const select=root.querySelector('select');
 const q=s=>root.querySelector(s);
 const player=stepper(root,{steps:()=>4,draw:(step)=>{
 const mode=select.value, hot=mode==='hot',full=mode==='full';
 q('[data-old]').parentElement.firstChild.textContent=step===3&&hot?'Item 1 · redirect → item 2 ':'Item 1 · P7 · 20.00 ';
 q('[data-old]').textContent=step===0?'current version':step===1?'replaced; writer uncommitted':step===2?'old report still sees 20.00':hot?'redirect can retain the chain':'obsolete once no snapshot needs it';
 q('[data-new]').hidden=step===0||full;
 q('[data-new]').textContent='Item 2 · P7 · 25.00'+(hot?' · heap-only':' · ordinary tuple');
 q('[data-page1]').hidden=step===0||!full;
 q('[data-index]').textContent=step===0||hot?'P7 → (0,1)':full?'P7 → (0,1) and (1,1)':'P7 → (0,1) and (0,2)';
 const common=['The primary-key entry leads to item 1. P7 costs 20.00.',hot?'The update fits on page 0 and price is not indexed. The new tuple is heap-only; the original entry reaches it through the old item.':full?'Page 0 has no room. The replacement goes to page 1. A new primary-key entry is required despite the unchanged product ID.':'Price is indexed. HOT is unavailable; ordinary indexes receive entries for the replacement, including the unchanged primary key.','The writer commits. A fresh snapshot reads 25.00; the earlier report still reads 20.00.',hot?'Once older snapshots no longer need the old tuple, pruning can replace its body with a redirect. The original index address still works.':'After older snapshots finish, cleanup can remove obsolete tuples and index entries. Commit alone does not remove them.'];
 q('[data-explanation]').textContent=common[step];
 }});
 select.addEventListener('change',()=>{q('[data-reset]').click();player.render()});
}
</script>
